description:
ADR-0071 makes every --json document a frozen, additive-only surface at 1.0, and the existing convention is a top-level schema_version (json_err.rs, host.rs, tag.rs, spatial.rs, workspace.rs, worktree.rs, launch.rs, plugin/json.rs, phux-mcp, phux-core::screen all carry one). Seven surfaces do not, so once frozen they have no way to signal a shape change:

  crates/phux/src/commands/new.rs:215     phux new --json     {session, terminal_id}
  crates/phux/src/commands/spawn.rs:356   phux spawn --json   {terminal_id, satellite}
  crates/phux/src/commands/ask.rs:74      phux ask --json     {event, terminal, id, question, suggestions, elapsed_seconds}
  crates/phux/src/commands/pair.rs:252    phux pair --json    {token, cert_fingerprint, overlay_addresses, ws_addr, quic_addr, connect_link, tokens_path}
  crates/phux/src/commands/play.rs:279    phux play --json    {terminal_id, path, cols, rows, events, speed, idle_limit, duration_ms, passes}
  crates/phux/src/commands/rec/mod.rs:420 phux rec --json     {path, format, bytes, duration_ms, frames, cols, rows}
  crates/phux/src/commands/watch.rs:179   phux watch --json   per-line NDJSON event objects {event, terminal, ...}

watch is the one that needs a design call rather than a one-line edit: it is a streaming NDJSON line protocol, so stamping schema_version on every line is wasteful and a versioned header line (or a documented decision that the stream is versioned by the binary) may be the better shape. Decide it explicitly rather than by default.

Adding the field is additive and safe today; it is a breaking change after 1.0. Do this before the freeze, not after.

MEASURED 2026-08-12 while building phux-cockpit-q4z.

Model.writeWorkspaceState does:

    if (std.fs.path.dirname(path)) |parent| cwd.createDirPath(io, parent) catch return;
    cwd.writeFile(io, ...) catch return;

createDirPath on a parent that is a SYMLINK to a directory fails on macOS, and the 'catch return' then swallows the whole write. Reproduced concretely in the settings-theme work: a config file at /tmp/<name> (where /tmp is a symlink to /private/tmp) never got written at all, while the identical code with the order reversed wrote it correctly. The test 'return keeps the previewed theme and writes it to the config file' in src/tests/settings_theme_tests.zig was RED on the createDirPath-first order and GREEN on the write-first order; that is the whole diff.

Model.writeConfigTheme (added in q4z) already does it the right way and carries the reasoning:

    if (cwd.writeFile(io, ...)) |_| return else |_| {}
    if (std.fs.path.dirname(path)) |parent| cwd.createDirPath(io, parent) catch return;
    cwd.writeFile(io, ...) catch return;

writeWorkspaceState was NOT changed in q4z: it is a different code path with its own tests, and changing it wants its own RED-first proof. The exposure is real but narrow — PHUX_COCKPIT_STATE pointed at a path under a symlinked parent, or a platform state directory reached through one. Fix is the same three lines plus a test that points PHUX_COCKPIT_STATE under /tmp and asserts the layout comes back.

design:
DECISION (recorded during the untangle session, before implementation):

Take the import-site alias convention, NOT the rename. Reasons, in order:

1. Renaming `ClientId` in phux-protocol is an API break on a **publishable** crate (phux-protocol is the only `publish = true` member). ADR-0061 makes that a version-breaking change. The payoff does not justify it.
2. Renaming the phux-server side instead avoids the API break but costs ~250 use sites across 12+ files (hub/relay.rs 61, terminal_actor/mod.rs 55, runtime/commands.rs 30, state/client_table.rs 27, runtime/client.rs 24, ...), landing in the middle of a concurrent refactor of that same crate. High conflict, low information gain: within phux-server, bare `ClientId` is already unambiguous.
3. The confusion is only real for a reader crossing the crate boundary — which is exactly what an import-site alias fixes, at the point of crossing.

The convention already exists in the codebase, applied ad hoc rather than as a rule. crates/phux-server/src/state/id_space.rs:32-34 is the model:

    use phux_core::ids::{SessionId, TerminalId, WindowId};
    use phux_protocol::ids::{
        SessionId as WireSessionId, TerminalId as WireTerminalId, WindowId as WireWindowId,
    };

Rule to write down: when a module imports a type from phux-protocol whose bare name is also defined in phux-core or in the importing crate, it MUST alias the protocol import with a `Wire` prefix. Applies to at minimum: ClientId, TerminalId, SessionId, WindowId, LayoutNode, SplitDir, WindowInfo, HistoryRejectionReason.

Home for the rule: CONTRIBUTING.md, "Additional expectations" section (line ~111). NOT docs/CONVENTIONS.md — that file governs the doc system (frontmatter, TL;DR, ADR template), not code style. The bead's original acceptance criterion named CONVENTIONS.md; that was wrong.

Remaining work beyond the convention: `AttachError` (phux-client/src/attach/driver.rs:782 vs phux-server/src/state/client.rs:85) is not import-aliased anywhere because the two are never imported together. It is a pure reader hazard. Lowest priority of the set; a doc comment on each pointing at the other may be the whole fix.

Spec ref: SPEC.md:1017-1048 §10.3 Layout.

Replace crates/phux-core/src/window.rs's placeholder LayoutNode with:

  pub enum LayoutNode {
      Leaf(PaneId),
      Split { dir: SplitDir, ratio: f32, left: Box<LayoutNode>, right: Box<LayoutNode> },
  }
  pub enum SplitDir { Horizontal, Vertical }

Operations on Window (or a free fn over LayoutNode):
  pub fn split_horizontal(&mut self, target: PaneId, new_pane: PaneId, ratio: f32);
  pub fn split_vertical(&mut self, target: PaneId, new_pane: PaneId, ratio: f32);
  pub fn kill_pane(&mut self, target: PaneId);  // collapse parent Split if it had this leaf
  pub fn focus_direction(&self, current: PaneId, dir: Direction) -> Option<PaneId>;

Direction enum: Up, Down, Left, Right (maps onto split tree traversal).

Compute pane rectangles given window dims:
  pub fn pane_rects(&self, window_dims: (u16, u16)) -> HashMap<PaneId, Rect>;
  pub struct Rect { pub x: u16, pub y: u16, pub w: u16, pub h: u16 }

Proptest in tests/layout.rs:
  - any sequence of split/kill ops leaves a valid tree (every PaneId in
    Window.panes has exactly one leaf; no orphan leaves)
  - pane_rects sums to window_dims exactly (no slop, accounting for split gutters if any)
  - focus_direction is a partial function (Option) — no panic on edge cases

